     "weathering",
     "erosion",
     "deposition",
     "meander",
     "delta",
     "glacier"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Exogenetic (external) processes work from outside on the surface. Weathering breaks rocks in place by temperature changes, water and plants. Erosion carries material away by running water, wind, glaciers and waves, and deposition leaves it elsewhere.\nRivers form V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, meanders, flood plains and deltas. Glaciers carve U-shaped valleys, wind makes dunes in deserts and waves make cliffs, caves and beaches.\nThese processes slowly shape the landscape and create fertile land."
    }
   },
